--- body ---
## Context

The author has reported for weeks that the sitter "tends to disappear on its
own from the installed-plugins list". Ticket 0688 investigated, ruled out both
mechanisms it had named (no Zotero update happened; the install used Tools ->
Add-ons -> Install Add-on From File, the persistent path), declined to invent
a third, and closed with the cause open and instrumentation added so the next
occurrence would leave evidence.

It occurred twice on the evening of 2026-09-06, during live verification, and
this time the evidence was collected while it was happening.

## Read the log before this body (corrected 2026-09-11)

Everything below is the ticket **as filed on 2026-09-06**. The log has
superseded it on three points, and a reader who takes the body at face value
will re-run refuted work. This section exists because that just happened.

1. **The mechanism in the title and in "The mechanism this points at" is
   REFUTED.** 0.2.15 shipped with `update.json` advertising a *compatible*
   version and died on the same schedule as the empty-list build, disabled at
   +20 s and deleted at +40 s. The timing never fitted either:
   `extensions.update.interval` is 86400, a daily check, against a disable
   twenty seconds after install. The title is kept only so the id stays
   greppable (log, 2026-09-06T22:34Z).
2. **Action 1 below cannot be run at all.** It asks for a build with
   `update_url` REMOVED. Settled with a control the same evening: `update_url`
   is REQUIRED — the identical build without it (0.2.14) is refused at install
   with "peut-etre incompatible avec cette version de Zotero". This also
   corrects 0688's docs-based reading that only `applications.zotero` need be
   present. Actions 2 and 3, which branch off Action 1's outcome, fall with it.
3. **Four deliberate arms have since been run and all survived** — first
   install, single replacement, pre-fix payload, disable/re-enable, across two
   machines. The ticket was closed as parked on 2026-09-08 and **reopened the
   same evening on a live recurrence**: five consecutive GUI replacements,
   0.3.15 through 0.3.19, into one process never restarted, over roughly an
   hour. Arm 2 tested exactly ONE replacement.

**The live candidate is therefore cumulative rapid replacement in a single long
session, and no bounded experiment has excluded plain intermittency.** That arm
needs many unattended cycles, which is what Action 4's instrument was built
for. Do not re-run arms 1 to 3: they were run deliberately, with controls, and
they answered.

## What was observed

One Zotero session, started 20:56:31, never restarted:

- 23:0x  `verify` reports `present, version 0.2.13, active False, location app-profile`
- 23:35:59  `extensions/` and `extensions.json` both mtime-stamped; the record
  is gone and `extensions/sdt-pack-sitter@....xpi` is deleted from disk
- The running instance kept working from memory throughout -- the author could
  still open and close the dialog, and the toolbar spinner still animated.

So: registered, then DISABLED, then REMOVED, file and all, mid-session, with no
Zotero update and no user action. Disable-then-remove is what Gecko's
AddonManager does to an add-on whose update/compatibility check concludes there
is no compatible version of it.

## The mechanism this points at

`manifest.json` carries `applications.zotero.update_url`, and Zotero fetches it
on a timer after startup -- which is the one thing in this plugin that happens
minutes into a session, unprompted, matching the observed timing.

That URL resolves (HTTP 200) and returns a well-formed Mozilla update manifest
naming this add-on with an EMPTY updates array:

    "addons": { "sdt-pack-sitter@search-works-for-zotero.invalid": { "updates": [] } }

The reading to test is that Zotero takes "your id is listed, and it has no
versions" as "there is no compatible version of you", disables, and cleans up.

**This is why 0688's fix did not stop it.** 0688 changed `update_url` from
`https://example.invalid/...` (unreachable -> no version information) to a
reachable manifest carrying an empty list (-> still no version information).
Both hand Zotero the same answer. The change was a real fix to a real defect --
a URL chosen to never resolve -- and it did not touch this mechanism, which
nobody had identified. `update.json`'s own comment says as much and says it
honestly: "No claim is made either way about what an empty list or an
unreachable update_url does to an installed add-on -- that was not established
against Zotero's own fork."

## Relevant files

- `plugins/sdt-sitter/manifest.json` - carries `applications.zotero.update_url`
- `plugins/sdt-sitter/update.json` - the empty-updates manifest it names
- `bench/sdt_sitter_install.py` - `verify` reads the host's own record, and is
  what distinguishes the three states this defect passes through
- `tickets/closed/0688-sitter-persistent-install-and-manifest-id.erg` - the
  investigation this continues; read its log before adding a fourth guess

## Actions

**Actions 1 to 3 are VOID as written — see "Read the log before this body".
`update_url` cannot be removed (the build is refused at install), and the
mechanism they isolate is refuted. They are left in place unstruck because the
reasoning that produced them is still worth reading, and because deleting a
refuted arm is how it gets proposed again.**

1. Isolate, one variable. Build the current payload with `update_url` REMOVED
   and nothing else changed, install it, and leave Zotero running past the
   window in which the removal has been seen (~40 min). Zotero's own developer
   docs list only `applications.zotero` itself as required; 0688 read them and
   recorded that `update_url` "is nowhere stated as required". 0688 deliberately
   kept it rather than change two variables at once on a plugin already
   disappearing -- correct then, and the live reproduction is what now justifies
   testing the other arm.
   Probe build: 0.2.14, `update_url` removed, identical otherwise.
2. If it survives, that is the mechanism. Decide the permanent shape: drop
   `update_url` (no update channel exists, so it advertises nothing anyone can
   install), or publish a real entry per release with `update_link` and version
   bounds. Do not leave an empty list.
3. If it does NOT survive, the mechanism is elsewhere and this ticket has
   narrowed it by one: record that, and do not close the ticket with a guess.
   The next candidate to examine is the `.invalid` id under a host that may
   validate ids it never dereferences.
4. ~~NEW, blocking 1-3 as filed: get a build installed at all, without GUI
   automation.~~ **DISCHARGED 2026-09-11.** Candidate (a) was built and merged:
   `bench/zotero_rdp_client.py` with `bench/zotero_arm_devtools_server.js`,
   ticket 0766, PR #523, against `--start-debugger-server`'s Remote Debugging
   Protocol. Candidate (b), asking the author to install by hand each cycle, is
   no longer needed and should not be asked for.

   Two defects were found in that client by its own review before it merged,
   and both are of this ticket's favourite family, the silent failure: replies
   were matched by actor name alone, so a late reply from a timed-out earlier
   call could be attributed to the next one (`d67ed60` matches by shape and
   ends the connection on timeout), and `eval_js` did not unwrap a promise
   result, swallowing a rejection (`fa9e89e`). Both are fixed on main. They are
   recorded here because they set the condition under which this client may be
   used as an instrument, in the Test section below.

## Test

**The instrument this section names is gone (corrected 2026-09-11).**
`scratchpad/sitter-watch.sh` ran — the log's 2026-09-06T22:34Z note reads six
cycles off `scratchpad/sitter-watch.log`, and it is what carried arms 1 to 3.
But `git log --all --diff-filter=A -- "*sitter-watch*"` finds it added on no
branch ever, and `scratchpad/` is not in the tree today. The evidence it
produced survives only as prose quoted in this log. Nothing is watching now, so
no arm can produce a verdict until it is rebuilt, whoever or whatever performs
the install.

This is the blocker for the remaining arm. The install is not: Action 4's
instrument exists and is merged.

Rebuild it, and commit it this time. A file a later workpackage consumes is
durable state, not scratch (`rules/git.md`, don't gitignore handoff artifacts).
What it must do is unchanged: poll the three facts 0688's three-state model
separates -- record present, record active, XPI on disk -- plus Zotero's pid,
and append a line ONLY on a change, so a quiet log reads "nothing moved" and a
line is a stamped transition.

Two conditions this ticket did not previously state:

- **A positive control before any quiet log is believed.** Provoke a transition
  deliberately -- disable the add-on by hand, or move the XPI -- and watch the
  watcher emit a line. A silent log and a blind watcher are the same artifact
  until one of them has been seen to fire.
- **The observation channel stays out of band from the RDP client.** The client
  may perform the install; it must not be what reports the resulting state.
  Both defects found in it before merge were silent failures, so a reading
  taken through it cannot distinguish a true state from a misattributed reply.
  `bench/sdt_sitter_install.py verify` reads the host's own record, and the
  manifest inside the installed `.xpi` on disk says whether `update_url` is
  really absent. Confirm the payload that way before starting the clock.

## Verification

- [ ] The probe build (no `update_url`) survives a Zotero session longer than
      any build carrying one has
- [ ] `sitter-watch.log` shows the survival as an absence of transitions, over
      a window that previously contained one
- [ ] A build WITH `update_url` restored disappears again -- the control that
      makes the survival mean something rather than a quiet evening

## Invariants

- Do not "fix" this by having the installer reinstall the plugin on a timer.
  That hides a host decision the plugin should not be arguing with, and would
  make the next occurrence invisible again.
- `bench/check_sitter_version.py`'s guard stands: any payload change needs its
  own version number, probe builds included.
- The verdict never travels through the instrument that performs the install.
  Action 1 is decided by out-of-band observation of the host's own state, which
  is why it does not inherit the RDP client's failure modes and why it is the
  cheap arm. The volume arm is the opposite case: many cycles, timeouts, and
  exactly the conditions under which a reply-matching defect would manufacture
  or erase the intermittency it is meant to measure.

## Exit criteria

The disappearance has a named, demonstrated mechanism and a fix whose control
arm has been run in both directions -- or, if `update_url` is exonerated, this
ticket records that with its evidence and hands on the next candidate.
